00:11:19,412 --> 00:11:32,412
In 1965, 17-year-old high school student Randy Garner stayed awake for 264 hours—that's 11 days—to see how he'd cope without sleep.

124
00:11:32,412 --> 00:11:35,412
On the second day, his eyes stopped focusing.

125
00:11:35,412 --> 00:11:39,412
Next, he lost the ability to identify objects by touch.

126
00:11:39,412 --> 00:11:42,412
By day three, Garner was moody and uncoordinated.

127
00:11:42,412 --> 00:11:47,412
At the end of the experiment, he was struggling to concentrate, had trouble with short-term memory,

128
00:11:47,412 --> 00:11:50,412
became paranoid, and started hallucinating.

129
00:11:50,412 --> 00:11:55,412
Although Garner recovered without long-term psychological or physical damage,

130
00:11:55,412 --> 00:12:00,412
for others, losing shut-eye can result in hormonal imbalance, illness,

131
00:12:00,412 --> 00:12:02,412
and in extreme cases, death.

132
00:12:02,412 --> 00:12:06,412
We're only beginning to understand why we sleep to begin with,

133
00:12:06,412 --> 00:12:08,412
but we do know it's essential.

134
00:12:08,412 --> 00:12:13,412
Adults need seven to eight hours of sleep a night, and adolescents need about ten.

135
00:12:13,412 --> 00:12:18,412
We grow sleepy due to signals from our body telling our brain we are tired,

136
00:12:18,412 --> 00:12:22,412
and signals from the environment telling us it's dark outside.

137
00:12:22,412 --> 00:12:28,412
The rise in sleep-inducing chemicals like adenosine and melatonin send us into a light doze

138
00:12:28,412 --> 00:12:34,412
that grows deeper, making our breathing and heart rate slow down, and our muscles relax.

139
00:12:34,412 --> 00:12:41,412
This non-REM sleep is when DNA is repaired, and our bodies replenish themselves for the day ahead.

140
00:12:41,412 --> 00:12:50,412
In the United States, it's estimated that 30% of adults and 66% of adolescents are regularly sleep-deprived.

141
00:12:50,412 --> 00:12:53,412
This isn't just a minor inconvenience.

142
00:12:53,412 --> 00:12:56,412
Staying awake can cause serious bodily harm.

143
00:12:56,412 --> 00:13:02,412
When we lose sleep, learning, memory, mood, and reaction time are affected.

144
00:13:02,412 --> 00:13:08,412
Sleeplessness may also cause inflammation, hallucinations, high blood pressure,

145
00:13:08,412 --> 00:13:12,412
and it's even been linked to diabetes and obesity.

146
00:13:12,412 --> 00:13:20,412
In 2014, a devoted soccer fan died after staying awake for 48 hours to watch the World Cup.

147
00:13:20,412 --> 00:13:26,412
While his untimely death was due to a stroke, studies show that chronically sleeping fewer than six hours a night

148
00:13:26,412 --> 00:13:35,412
increases stroke risk by four and a half times compared to those getting a consistent seven to eight hours of shut-eye.

149
00:13:35,412 --> 00:13:40,412
For a handful of people on the planet who carry a rare inherited genetic mutation,

150
00:13:40,412 --> 00:13:42,412
sleeplessness is a daily reality.

151
00:13:42,412 --> 00:13:49,412
This condition, known as fatal familial insomnia, places the body in a nightmarish state of wakefulness,

152
00:13:49,412 --> 00:13:53,412
forbidding it from entering the sanctuary of sleep.

153
00:13:53,412 --> 00:13:59,412
Within months or years, this progressively worsening condition leads to dementia and death.

154
00:13:59,412 --> 00:14:03,412
How can sleep deprivation cause such immense suffering?

155
00:14:03,412 --> 00:14:08,412
Scientists think the answer lies with the accumulation of waste products in the brain.

156
00:14:08,412 --> 00:14:13,412
During our waking hours, our cells are busy using up our day's energy sources,

157
00:14:13,412 --> 00:14:18,412
which get broken down into various byproducts including adenosine.

158
00:14:18,412 --> 00:14:25,412
As adenosine builds up, it increases the urge to sleep, also known as sleep pressure.

159
00:14:25,412 --> 00:14:30,412
In fact, caffeine works by blocking adenosine's receptor pathways.

160
00:14:30,412 --> 00:14:33,412
Other waste products also build up in the brain,

161
00:14:33,412 --> 00:14:37,412
and if they're not cleared away, they collectively overload the brain

162
00:14:37,412 --> 00:14:42,412
and are thought to lead to the many negative symptoms of sleep deprivation.

163
00:14:42,412 --> 00:14:46,412
So what's happening in our brain when we sleep to prevent this?

164
00:14:46,412 --> 00:14:49,412
Scientists found something called the glymphatic system,

165
00:14:49,412 --> 00:14:55,412
a cleanup mechanism that removes this buildup and is much more active when we're asleep.

166
00:14:55,412 --> 00:15:03,412
It works by using cerebrospinal fluid to flush away toxic byproducts that accumulate between cells.

167
00:15:03,412 --> 00:15:07,412
Lymphatic vessels, which serve as pathways for immune cells,

168
00:15:07,412 --> 00:15:10,412
have recently been discovered in the brain,

169
00:15:10,412 --> 00:15:15,412
and they may also play a role in clearing out the brain's daily waste products.

170
00:15:15,412 --> 00:15:16,412
and they may be able to use the same way to prevent this.

171
00:15:16,412 --> 00:15:28,412
They may be able to use the same type of brain.

172
00:15:16,788 --> 00:15:25,788
While scientists continue exploring the restorative mechanisms behind sleep, we can be sure that slipping into slumber is a necessity.

173
00:15:25,788 --> 00:15:29,788
If we want to maintain our health and our sanity.

